Zitron says, wouldn't just be a recession.

284
00:08:31,354 --> 00:08:32,504
It would be a tech depression.

285
00:08:32,917 --> 00:08:34,867
These companies make up a huge share of

286
00:08:34,879 --> 00:08:35,654
the stock market,

287
00:08:36,042 --> 00:08:38,629
so he expects people's retirement savings to fall

288
00:08:38,642 --> 00:08:39,892
twenty to forty percent,

289
00:08:40,217 --> 00:08:41,429
and not bounce back quickly.

290
00:08:41,842 --> 00:08:42,492
To be clear,

291
00:08:42,804 --> 00:08:44,279
this is one man's forecast.

292
00:08:44,729 --> 00:08:47,767
Bartlett points out that previous technologies also lost

293
00:08:47,779 --> 00:08:49,254
money before they paid off,

294
00:08:49,517 --> 00:08:51,492
from Amazon Web Services to Uber.

295
00:08:51,904 --> 00:08:52,817
Zitron's reply:

296
00:08:53,204 --> 00:08:54,642
none of them lost this much,

297
00:08:54,867 --> 00:08:55,617
this fast.

298
00:08:56,029 --> 00:08:57,179
So what would it take to change his

299
00:08:57,192 --> 00:08:57,567
mind?

300
00:08:58,079 --> 00:08:58,679
Part six.

301
00:08:59,092 --> 00:09:00,004
What would prove him wrong?

302
00:09:00,417 --> 00:09:01,804
Remember the question from the start?

303
00:09:02,229 --> 00:09:03,492
Bartlett asks it directly.

304
00:09:03,904 --> 00:09:04,892
What would it take for you to be

305
00:09:04,904 --> 00:09:05,242
wrong?

306
00:09:05,654 --> 00:09:06,442
Zitron's answer:

307
00:09:06,892 --> 00:09:08,879
a hardware breakthrough that cuts the cost of

308
00:09:08,892 --> 00:09:11,242
AI by something like a thousand times.

309
00:09:11,654 --> 00:09:13,267
A product that works on its own,

310
00:09:13,667 --> 00:09:16,067
reliably, without the complicated setup.

311
00:09:16,479 --> 00:09:17,142
In his words,

312
00:09:17,404 --> 00:09:20,129
it would have to be indistinguishable from magic.

313
00:09:20,554 --> 00:09:22,129
And he says the bar is that high

314
00:09:22,142 --> 00:09:24,204
because the companies set it there themselves.

315
00:09:24,617 --> 00:09:26,067
So what should ordinary people do?

316
00:09:26,479 --> 00:09:27,429
Zitron is careful.

317
00:09:27,854 --> 00:09:29,867
He says he isn't giving financial advice,

318
00:09:30,154 --> 00:09:31,954
and that he personally keeps his money in

319
00:09:31,967 --> 00:09:32,542
cash.

320
00:09:32,954 --> 00:09:34,592
His advice is about attitude:

321
00:09:34,854 --> 00:09:36,717
be suspicious of tech promises.

322
00:09:37,129 --> 00:09:37,754
Watch the wording.

323
00:09:38,179 --> 00:09:41,079
When a company reports an annualized run rate,

324
00:09:41,354 --> 00:09:41,879
he says,

325
00:09:42,304 --> 00:09:43,829
ask what that actually means,

326
00:09:44,029 --> 00:09:45,442
because they rarely define it.

327
00:09:45,854 --> 00:09:47,779
And one piece of advice he says he

328
00:09:47,792 --> 00:09:48,504
lives by:

329
00:09:48,929 --> 00:09:50,779
every con begins by rushing you.

330
00:09:51,192 --> 00:09:52,979
Unless something is truly on fire,

331
00:09:53,267 --> 00:09:53,892
slow down.

332
00:09:54,317 --> 00:09:55,704
Bartlett ends with balance.

333
00:09:56,129 --> 00:09:57,767
He says he doesn't fully agree,

334
00:09:58,129 --> 00:10:00,229
but that voices like Zitron's force him to

335
00:10:00,242 --> 00:10:01,017
think harder,

336
00:10:01,392 --> 00:10:03,079
and that the best approach is to collect

337
00:10:03,092 --> 00:10:06,029
many perspectives and follow the evidence yourself.

338
00:10:06,454 --> 00:10:08,867
Zitron's last words aren't about money at all.

339
00:10:09,292 --> 00:10:10,717
He says the best part of being a

340
00:10:10,729 --> 00:10:12,379
critic has been the people around him.

341
00:10:12,804 --> 00:10:14,142
So tell the people you love that you

342
00:10:14,154 --> 00:10:14,592
love them.

343
00:10:15,004 --> 00:10:17,217
Tell the writers and creators you enjoy that

344
00:10:17,229 --> 00:10:18,117
their work matters.

345
00:10:18,542 --> 00:10:20,092
This was two and a half hours in

346
00:10:20,104 --> 00:10:20,792
ten minutes.

347
00:10:21,217 --> 00:10:23,029
The full conversation goes deeper,

348
00:10:23,304 --> 00:10:24,379
into Google search,

349
00:10:24,654 --> 00:10:26,279
robots, and agents.

350
